 - **Type:** two faces. Archivo for titles and the lead figure of a view (a token price, a wallet balance), and it is only itself with its width axis set — anything in the display face carries `font-variation-settings: var(--pk-display-wdth)`, which `.pk-display`, `h1` and `h2` already do. Instrument Sans for everything else, including every control label; where a monospace used to carry a chip, a ticker or a small label, that is this face at 10–12px tracked 0.04em to 0.14em, and where it carried figures it is this face with `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`.
 - **Buttons:** one `primary` per view, and never two filled buttons side by side — pair `primary` with `secondary`. `tile` is the rectangular action used in studio grids. Sizes are `sm`, `md` and `lg`; `mono` switches the label to uppercase mono for navigation and utility actions.
 - **Icons:** `Icon` wraps a curated set from `lucide-react`, which matches what the canvases drew by hand: a 24×24 grid, round joins and one stroke weight (1.9 here). Colour comes from `currentColor`, and `label` is required whenever the icon is the only content of a control. Add a name to `src/icon.tsx` rather than importing from `lucide-react` inside a feature — that is how two screens end up with two different arrows.
-- **Colour:** every colour, the ink ramp, the hairlines and the three faces are declared once in `src/styles.css`, per surface. Feature stylesheets alias them (`--ink: var(--pk-ink)`) and never hold a literal, which is what let the landing and the public pages fade text to two different greys before.
+- **Colour:** every colour, the ink ramp, the hairlines and the three faces are declared once in `src/styles.css`, per surface, and it is the only file that may hold a colour literal or define a `--pk-*` property. Its own rules read tokens too: a literal is allowed inside a token's definition and nowhere else. Feature stylesheets read the tokens by name (`var(--pk-ink)`); they define no alias of one and hold no literal. `src/tokens.ts` holds the five values code must hold as values (Next's `themeColor`, the chart's fallbacks), keyed by token name and pinned to `styles.css` by `scripts/design/mirror.test.mjs`. `src/brand.tsx` holds a third party's logo colours, which are not tokens and carry `data-third-party-mark` so the design checks skip them. `npm run design:source` enforces all of it.
 - **Panel:** the surface content sits on — one border, one radius, one translucent ground — lives as `.pk-panel` in the stylesheet. A feature sheet takes it with `composes: pk-panel from global` and keeps only its own difference (a blur, a lit top edge, its padding), rather than declaring the border and the radius again.
 - **Fields:** `Field` is the frame — a mono micro label, the control, and one line under it that carries a hint or an error through `tone`. `Input` and `Textarea` are the controls; `prefix` puts a fixed mark inside the frame, like the @ before a handle, so the two read as one. A field lights on focus in `--pk-focus`, which is cyan on the public surface and lime in the studio. `shape="pill"` is for a search box sitting in a row of filters.
 - **Chips:** one object, three renderings. `Chip` presses (a filter, a copy action), `ChipLink` navigates (a contract, an X handle, an agent's page), `ChipText` only reads (a market cap, a venue). `Tag` is the louder cousin for a status the eye should catch first.
 - **Segmented:** each option carries `data-value`, so a feature can tint one of them differently — Ask violet and Trade lime inside one track — without counting children in CSS. `Segmented` is the exclusive choice always on screen — buy or sell, grid or table. `tone="lime"` is for taking a side, `ink` for a view of the same data. A filter that can be off is a `Chip`; an action that does something is a `Button`.
 - **Meter:** `Meter` is the filled track — graduation progress, a reputation score, a trait. Tone says what the figure beside it already says: `lime` for landed or graduated, `amber` for on its way, `magenta` for against, `cyan` for neutral. It is announced only with a `label`; without one it is decoration repeating a number that is already written.
-- **Charts:** `CandleChart` and `BarChart` wrap [lightweight-charts](https://github.com/tradingview/lightweight-charts). Features pass data — candles or bars, each bucket timestamped in milliseconds — and the look is decided in `src/chart.tsx`, which reads `--pk-ink-faint`, `--pk-border`, `--pk-win`, `--pk-loss` and `--pk-font` from the surrounding surface, so a chart follows `data-surface` like everything else. Do not call `createChart` in a feature; add an option or a series type here instead. The library's in-panel logo is turned off (`layout.attributionLogo: false`), which its Apache-2.0 notice allows as long as the credit and a link to <https://www.tradingview.com/> live on the page — `PublicShell` carries them in its footer on the routes that pass `charts`, which is the two that mount one — so a page that adds a chart adds that prop, and a page that does not must not claim a credit it never earned.
